  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 113 AM EDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nQuiet weather to persist through the end of the week. A 130+ kt \nupper level jet and attendant PV anomaly passes through the Great \nLakes later today. However, we remain in the unfavorable right exit \nregion of this jet streak. Better CVA also passes to the north and \nlags well behind the associated surface front. Modest return flow \nahead of this front will push highs near 80F (particularly in our \nS/SW) but surface dewpoints remain in the 50s and forecast soundings \nshow very dry/stable and capped profiles. No precip expected with \nthis frontal passage and likely just some high-based clouds at times \nthrough tonight. Another broad low level anticyclone then settles \ninto the Upper Midwest on Thursday on the downstream side of large \nwestern CONUS ridge. Highs on Thu drop back to around 70F with \ndewpoints around 40F. Surface high passes over our area Thu night \nand return flow starts again by late Friday. Several deterministic \n00Z models generate some light QPF along the associated warm front/ \ntheta-e surge but NBM is holding dry. Wouldn't be surprised if we \nhave to add a low chance PoP or sprinkle mention Fri night but will \nhold off for now.\n\nAttention then turns to the remnants of what will likely become \nHurricane Isaias in the Gulf over the next few days. Latest 00Z \nguidance showing a westward shift in the associated rain shield that \nnow clips much of our area with at least some light rain on Sunday. \nNBM PoP's now over 60% for much of our SE CWA. Still not expecting \nany appreciable impacts from this event though. Even a widespread 1-\n1.5\" should be tolerable given recent dry conditions and that is \ndefinitely on the high-end of possible outcomes. QPF amounts could \neasily be much lower. Some breezy conditions also possible but again \nnot strong enough to be impactful.",
